WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.770
Narrator: In "Jurassic
Park"'s famous kitchen scene,

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.879
this shot featured a man
in a full raptor suit.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.080
While the raptors in this
shot were created digitally.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.270
These shots featured raptor puppets

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.300
and actors wearing
raptor boots and gloves.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.220
You know that great moment
where the raptor reaches in,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.232
opens the door?

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.650
That's just a guy wearing a raptor glove

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.120
and reaching around.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.585
Narrator: The "Jurassic" movies
have constantly innovated

00:00:29.585 --> 00:00:31.770
how dinosaurs appear on-screen,

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.883
pushing CGI forward
while never exclusively

00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:38.300
relying on digital methods, even in 2022.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.460
We spoke with nine of the minds

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.620
behind the "Jurassic"
creatures to find out

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.600
how dinosaur effects have evolved

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.613
over the course of three decades.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.092
Steven Spielberg first
envisioned his dinosaurs

00:00:52.092 --> 00:00:54.750
as entirely stop-motion creations

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.337
designed by legendary stop-motion
animator Phil Tippett.

00:00:58.337 --> 00:00:59.920
How'd you do this?

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.470
I'll show you.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.450
They didn't want to initially trust

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.770
that we could do full-CG T. rexes,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.160
because he was the most
important critter in there.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.420
All the other ones could kind of

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:16.920
wander around in the background,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.710
but as far as the actors acting to that,

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:20.880
that was the big one.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.082
So we got the test shot
that we showed to Steven,

00:01:24.082 --> 00:01:25.625
and that's what changed the film.

00:01:25.625 --> 00:01:28.262
Narrator: Jean was there for
one of these initial tests.

00:01:28.262 --> 00:01:30.640
Jean: Suddenly, you went
from things that were

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.630
like a skeleton or the
Mercury Man in "T2,"

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.550
you went to something that had color

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.080
and texture and a sheen to it.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.730
You could put anything anywhere

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.222
on the screen and make it alive.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.730
Narrator: The only problem:
It looked like the manpower

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:51.570
needed on the computer-animation
side would be too high.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.417
So the stop-motion
artists at Tippett Studios

00:01:54.417 --> 00:01:56.747
worked with the VFX team at ILM

00:01:56.747 --> 00:02:00.690
to develop dinosaur
input devices, or DIDs,

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:03.990
physical models of the dinosaurs
hooked up to computers.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.867
The skeletons were covered with sensors

00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:08.292
that tracked the
orientations of their joints

00:02:08.292 --> 00:02:10.590
and sent that information to the computer,

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:13.350
translating the movement into key frames.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.750
Traditional stop-motion
animators could then work

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.550
with the computer models without having

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.920
to master computer-animation software,

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.468
shifting their skills
to the digital realm.

00:02:22.468 --> 00:02:24.534
Phil: That was the
thing that really melted

00:02:24.534 --> 00:02:27.750
the glacial divide between
traditional stop motion

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:29.731
and computer graphics.

00:02:29.731 --> 00:02:32.213
Narrator: Thanks to CG,
"Jurassic Park"'s T. rex

00:02:32.213 --> 00:02:34.290
could go from being a background character

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.390
to a star of the film.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.390
Spielberg even rewrote the
ending to include this fight

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.510
between the CG T. rex and two raptors.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.450
The real secret to the
dinosaur's believability, though?

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.650
When we first meet the T.
Rex, in the paddock sequence,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.820
it is a combination of
CG and practical effects.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.280
Narrator: Designed by
FX pioneer Stan Winston

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.120
and his team of sculptors,
puppeteers, and mechanics.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.510
Matt: That sequence cuts
between both methodologies.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.826
If you intercut CG with an actual,

00:03:06.826 --> 00:03:09.309
physical on-set puppet,

00:03:09.309 --> 00:03:12.695
they're never quite sure
what they're looking at.

00:03:12.695 --> 00:03:16.260
That is the reason that
that remains, to this day,

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:20.233
one of the more realistic fantasy
moments in cinema history.

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:21.468
Narrator: The animatronic dinosaurs

00:03:21.468 --> 00:03:22.980
had their own advancements.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.325
Matt: The dinosaurs of "Jurassic Park"

00:03:25.325 --> 00:03:29.100
required a whole rethink
on the mold-making process.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.898
Traditionally in practical effects,

00:03:30.898 --> 00:03:33.570
it would be done with
various types of stones.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:36.903
These would not be appropriate materials

00:03:36.903 --> 00:03:38.940
for gigantic molds.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:42.306
So the studio looked to the epoxies

00:03:42.306 --> 00:03:44.922
that were being used in
the aerospace industry

00:03:44.922 --> 00:03:48.027
and applied those materials
for the dinosaurs.

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:50.310
It allowed them to create very large,

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:54.900
very durable molds that
would retain their integrity.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.070
They wouldn't warp.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.570
Narrator: Like these
raptor puppets seen in

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.890
the attack on Muldoon.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.920
Matt: I was actually the
raptor left-arm puppeteer

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.417
in most sequences. The
arm cable-actuated devices

00:04:05.417 --> 00:04:09.030
allowed for fantastic
expressiveness with their hands.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.790
Narrator: And for the iconic,
complex kitchen sequence?

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.940
Matt: That sequence involves
every possible trick

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.310
in the book to make you
believe raptors are real.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:20.760
CGI raptors, insert
puppets that were basically

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.380
the head and a bit of the neck,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.380
lower-body rigs that were just the legs,

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:26.755
raptor gloves.

00:04:26.755 --> 00:04:30.510
It includes men in raptor
suits when they first

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:33.906
enter the kitchen and one
of them rears up and hoots.

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:36.420
[raptor calls]

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.670
That's a man in a suit.
The men-in-suit approach

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.650
was great for wider shots like that.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.172
But when you're close up,

00:04:42.172 --> 00:04:44.310
more often than not, it will be a puppet.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.320
Narrator: Ultimately,
there were 15 minutes

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.030
of dinosaurs in "Jurassic Park."

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.340
Matt: Nine minutes are practical puppets;

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.020
six minutes are CGI.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.445
Stan was a huge believer

00:04:54.445 --> 00:04:57.444
that the best way to fool an audience

00:04:57.444 --> 00:05:00.060
is don't lean too
heavily on one technique.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.368
Keep them guessing.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:05.753
Narrator: By the time the
second "Jurassic" movie

00:05:05.753 --> 00:05:08.220
rolled around, practical
innovations meant the team

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.980
could lean even more on animatronics.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:12.870
Matt: On "JP II," they changed it up.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.510
They wanted the skins to
move more realistically,

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:18.660
and so they created an elaborate network

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.820
of foam musculature.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.270
And on top of those
foam muscles that moved

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.280
and intersected very realistically,

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.020
they laid a much thinner skin,

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.180
so that you could actually get a sense

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.030
of the musculature moving
underneath the skin.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:34.512
Narrator: Plus, Stan Winston's mechanics

00:05:34.512 --> 00:05:36.727
extended their use of hydraulic motors

00:05:36.727 --> 00:05:39.000
used on the T. rex in the first movie.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.358
Matt: Even though the Tyrannosaurus rex

00:05:41.358 --> 00:05:43.983
from "Jurassic Park I" was by far

00:05:43.983 --> 00:05:46.850
the toughest character to pull off,

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:50.070
it actually was the easiest to puppeteer,

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.833
because it was a hydraulic puppet

00:05:52.833 --> 00:05:54.967
puppeteered via a telemetry device,

00:05:54.967 --> 00:05:57.720
so it only took a few people
to puppeteer that big T. rex.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:01.470
So that technique was what
we used for every dinosaur

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.440
in "Jurassic Park II," other
than the smallest of them.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.960
And thanks to that approach, the raptors,

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.851
which in the first one took 18 of us

00:06:09.851 --> 00:06:12.872
at times to operate, only
took two or three people

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:15.060
to operate in "Jurassic Park II."

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.100
Narrator: The smallest
dinosaurs still relied

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.810
on more traditional puppetry,

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.120
like the Compsognathus, or "compies,"

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:23.850
chicken-sized dinosaurs
that attack in packs,

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:26.820
most notably when they
team up to kill the hunter.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:29.302
Dennis: There were so many
of them, and so many CG ones

00:06:29.302 --> 00:06:30.893
had to jump on this actor.

00:06:30.893 --> 00:06:32.280
And they all had to be performing,

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.140
and the guy's moving
like this and thrashing,

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.922
and he's pulling them, and all the weights

00:06:35.922 --> 00:06:38.430
when they jump onto this
moving guy had to be right.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.110
Narrator: Stan Winston's team
built several types of puppets

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:43.860
to perform a range of compy behaviors.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.863
One type of puppet attached to the actor

00:06:45.863 --> 00:06:48.150
via Velcro pads on a hidden suit.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.257
Superstrong fishing line
rigged the puppet up

00:06:50.257 --> 00:06:51.980
to an off-screen controller,

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:55.440
which a puppeteer could tug
on to create a pecking motion.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.495
Meanwhile, an airline
trigger ran air through

00:06:57.495 --> 00:07:00.810
the compy's tail, causing it
to whip around in a frenzy.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.105
For this shot of Stormare
trying to grab and pull

00:07:03.105 --> 00:07:05.070
the compies off, the team used a puppet

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:07.710
with high-powered magnets on its feet.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.475
These magnets attached to other magnets

00:07:09.475 --> 00:07:11.113
sewn into the actor's clothes,

00:07:11.113 --> 00:07:13.530
making the compy difficult to pull off.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:16.020
The puppeteers could also
be as close to the puppets

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:18.957
as needed, since they could
just be erased in post.

00:07:18.957 --> 00:07:22.390
The length of the puppeteering rods

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:25.050
before digital had to be really long.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:26.340
But if you can't get close to the puppets,

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.680
you can't really do much
in terms of controlling it.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.685
It's always going to have
this kind of bouncing,

00:07:30.685 --> 00:07:32.310
kind of floaty kind of quality.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:35.268
Now, with digital, you
can choke up on that rod.

00:07:35.268 --> 00:07:37.560
You can be just a couple
inches from the puppet,

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.780
and you can be moving it
around really smoothly.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:43.180
The prime example is the
sequence where the little girl

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:45.810
and the compy interact there on the beach.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:47.610
The puppeteers were right there.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:50.270
They were within just a
foot or two of that compy.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:51.512
Narrator: In the final shots,

00:07:51.512 --> 00:07:54.210
the team mixed puppet
compies with digital ones,

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.220
which you can spot based on movement.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:59.490
Mainly the ones that seem
to have a lot of pizzazz

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:01.350
and a lot of anger, I
think, are the ones that we

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.870
could animate enough to
make them look interesting.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:05.760
Narrator: These mini
dinosaurs set the stage

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:09.060
for a much larger creature
in "Jurassic III,"

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:11.430
which would demand the heaviest, biggest,

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:14.400
and fastest dinosaur robot ever built.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:18.541
Joe: We wanted a dinosaur
that could defeat T. rex.

00:08:18.541 --> 00:08:21.763
You know, T. rex is
everybody's favorite dinosaur.

00:08:21.763 --> 00:08:23.490
Narrator: The movie's
paleontology consultant,

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.920
Jack Horner, suggested the Spinosaurus,

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:28.054
which was bigger and more vicious,

00:08:28.054 --> 00:08:31.103
capable of killing the T.
rex in the final showdown.

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:36.608
The foundation of that final
showdown was practical,

00:08:36.608 --> 00:08:39.157
filmed with two massive animatronics,

00:08:39.157 --> 00:08:42.870
as demonstrated here in
an early hand-puppet test.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:44.238
But filmmakers couldn't have

00:08:44.238 --> 00:08:46.620
the two puppets really go at it.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.020
They would've destroyed
each other pretty quickly.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:50.553
Narrator: So they storyboarded a fight

00:08:50.553 --> 00:08:52.772
that used an animatronic Spinosaurus

00:08:52.772 --> 00:08:55.121
and a digital T. Rex in some shots,

00:08:55.121 --> 00:08:56.912
and vice versa in others.

00:08:56.912 --> 00:08:58.900
Joe: That, I think, was the first time

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.625
in "Jurassic Park" movies that they were

00:09:00.625 --> 00:09:03.480
actually interacting with
each other physically.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.157
Narrator: For the
animatronics, the crew took

00:09:05.157 --> 00:09:07.290
one of the T. rexes
from the previous movie

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:10.220
out of storage and
refurbished it for the fight.

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:13.290
But the Spinosaurus had
to be built from scratch.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:14.882
Matt: The audience had to believe

00:09:14.882 --> 00:09:17.130
that this dinosaur could kill a T. rex.

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.040
So it had to be bigger,
it had to be faster.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:24.270
The T. rex had about 200
horsepower driving it.

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:27.270
The Spinosaurus had 1,000 horsepower.

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:30.120
It was five times more powerful.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:33.330
It was far sturdier
than the T. rex as well,

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.450
which had actually been quite delicate.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:37.530
Narrator: Once the
final Spinosaurus design

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:40.465
got a thumbs-up from a
consulting paleontologist,

00:09:40.465 --> 00:09:42.450
the team sculpted an 8-foot-long,

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:44.430
one-fifth-scale maquette.

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.404
Instead of sculpting the
animatronic from clay,

00:09:46.404 --> 00:09:47.979
like they did for the T. rex,

00:09:47.979 --> 00:09:50.490
the crew went with a
more high-tech method.

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:52.510
They used a cyberscan of the maquette

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:55.023
to create computer-milled foam pieces.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:58.020
And all these pieces had to withstand

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:00.390
a very specific element:

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:01.536
water.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:05.670
Cut! Good, good.

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.385
Matt: In "Jurassic Park I," the T. rex

00:10:07.385 --> 00:10:09.720
was rained upon extensively.

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:12.665
The foam skins acted like a sponge

00:10:12.665 --> 00:10:15.723
and absorbed tons of water in every shot,

00:10:15.723 --> 00:10:18.139
which affected its performance

00:10:18.139 --> 00:10:21.300
because it was finely
tuned to a certain weight,

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:23.040
but when all the water was absorbed,

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.590
it changed all those calculations.

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:29.850
They waterproofed the T.
rexes in "Jurassic Park II"

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:32.760
with a layer of silicone
over the foam skin.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:35.017
And that waterproofing approach

00:10:35.017 --> 00:10:37.433
reached its pinnacle with the Spinosaurus.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:42.794
Which, the inner mechanical devices

00:10:42.794 --> 00:10:45.420
were waterproofed in waterproof housings.

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:49.230
Aspects of the Spinosaurus'
face were actually cast

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.770
in a solid urethane that
would not absorb water.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:54.420
That, to this day, remains

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.461
the Guinness Book of World Records

00:10:56.461 --> 00:10:59.700
largest animatronic ever built for a film.

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:02.195
Narrator: It was so large, it
had to be lifted with cranes

00:11:02.195 --> 00:11:04.530
and driven to Universal Studios on a route

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.630
that didn't go under bridges.

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:08.160
Once on set, the crew mounted it

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.710
to a motorized cart that ran on tracks,

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:13.890
which allowed the animatronic
to move forward and backward

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:16.440
while it moved up and down on cylinders.

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:18.840
And to get those fast
movements you see here,

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:22.512
the Spinosaurus used
state-of-the-art hot-rod hydraulics

00:11:22.512 --> 00:11:25.410
controlled by an 18-inch telemetry device.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:28.110
William H. Macy, who played
Paul Kirby in the film,

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:29.560
said the puppet could turn its head

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:31.793
at twice the force of gravity,

00:11:31.793 --> 00:11:35.385
with the tip of its nose
moving at 100 miles per hour.

00:11:35.385 --> 00:11:38.220
Its strength didn't make
it indestructible, though.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:39.917
Joe: That scene with the airplane,

00:11:39.917 --> 00:11:42.150
every take, it would
lose one of its claws.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:44.850
Every take was a, "All
right, go back to the shop."

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:46.320
Narrator: When filming the big fight,

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:49.440
the two teams of puppeteers
used their telemetry devices

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:51.150
to swing the puppets around.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:54.088
Joe: That took about 12
animatronic operators

00:11:54.088 --> 00:11:55.740
to do each one of them.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:57.690
Narrator: Although they tried
to keep physical contact

00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:59.490
between the two puppets to a minimum,

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:01.380
the T. rex still got hurt.

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.420
Joe: It was actually
before the fight started.

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:06.630
It was the two animatronic
dinosaurs sort of roaring

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:09.390
at each other, T. rex sort
of started going crazy.

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:11.007
The puppet was just sort of vibrating,

00:12:11.007 --> 00:12:12.932
and it turns out that one
of the hydraulic lines

00:12:12.932 --> 00:12:16.803
had broken inside and hydraulic
fluid was going everywhere.

00:12:16.803 --> 00:12:19.080
And re-skin part of it and try to

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:20.490
sew it back together and everything.

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:22.680
It was wild. It's on film somewhere.

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:24.900
Narrator: 14 years
later, major improvements

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:27.420
in performance capture
made complex movement

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:30.990
like this possible in the
second "Jurassic" franchise,

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.750
a step up from puppets and stop motion.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.300
"Jurassic World" sought
to bring its velociraptors

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.850
to a whole new level of realism,

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:42.570
giving them an unprecedented
complexity of motion.

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:45.270
It was difficult for a
performance-capture artist

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.367
to physically stand the way that a raptor

00:12:47.367 --> 00:12:50.100
is supposed to for a
prolonged period of time.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:52.830
And velociraptors do have a unique size

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:55.200
and shape compared to human performers.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:58.920
But ILM was able to retarget
the captured performances

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.620
of the human actors to the raptor bodies

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:04.590
without losing the subtlety
of the performances.

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:06.982
The reward was that the raptors look heavy

00:13:06.982 --> 00:13:10.474
and lifelike with very
intricate movement on-screen.

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:15.510
With CG having advanced to this level,

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:18.660
the next film could go beyond pure realism

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:21.960
to give life to a genetically
engineered dinosaur

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:25.627
that never actually
existed: the Indoraptor.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.567
Unsurprisingly, this massive dinosaur

00:13:30.567 --> 00:13:32.970
would mainly be a digital creation.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:35.760
But the actors needed
something to react to on set.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:38.370
Neal: There was a lot
of physical interaction

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.640
between the actors and the
dinosaurs in "Fallen Kingdom,"

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:44.807
more so maybe than in the previous films.

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:47.907
Narrator: The solution, for some scenes,

00:13:47.907 --> 00:13:50.474
came in the form of partial animatronics,

00:13:50.474 --> 00:13:52.860
like this giant mechanical arm

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:54.601
and a moving, breathing head

00:13:54.601 --> 00:13:56.842
operated by a team of puppeteers,

00:13:56.842 --> 00:13:59.576
who controlled the tiny
movements in the eyes.

00:13:59.576 --> 00:14:01.475
Neal: A good deal of the Indoraptor

00:14:01.475 --> 00:14:04.650
was performed using a
real-time animatronic.

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:06.780
Narrator: The crew also
built a nonmoving head.

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:10.020
Neal: We created a full-size Indoraptor

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:12.630
head and neck and tail that was made

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:15.270
from a very lightweight
inflatable material,

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:18.270
so we actually blew it up
like a child's bouncy castle,

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:21.210
made a rig for it to allow
two performers to wear,

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:23.310
one the front end and one the back end.

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:26.130
We would use these inflatable versions

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:29.610
in any scene where the
actors needed an eyeline

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:33.630
or visual effects needed
a scale representation

00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:34.920
that wouldn't be in the frame.

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:36.810
Narrator: These various
forms of the Indoraptor

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:40.260
not only provided the cast
with tangible scene partners,

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:42.514
they also gave the camera
team something physical

00:14:42.514 --> 00:14:45.167
to work with and the VFX team a reference

00:14:45.167 --> 00:14:47.460
for the Indo's lighting and movement.

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:49.650
Neal: The movement of the
Indoraptor seemed to me

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:51.840
to be quite faithfully followed.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:53.940
I'm never really quite sure
whether there was a paint

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:56.490
overlay or whether or not
there is a complete scene

00:14:56.490 --> 00:14:59.100
substitution for the
Indoraptor in the end.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:00.990
I find it very difficult
to know where and when

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.150
the technique begins. It's great.

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:04.830
Narrator: On "Jurassic World: Dominion,"

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:06.930
decades of innovation made it possible

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:09.911
to create hybrid dinosaurs
that were part-CG,

00:15:09.911 --> 00:15:12.360
part-puppet, like the Giganotosaurus,

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:15.180
a dinosaur with a head the size of a car.

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:17.850
The head, roughly just
above the shoulders,

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:20.700
was an animatronic, and the
rest of the creature is digital.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:22.610
And if you can see where it's slightly

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:24.870
more opaque white around
the head and the neck,

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:26.853
that's the physical piece of the dinosaur

00:15:26.853 --> 00:15:28.710
that was going to be
made as an animatronic.

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:30.190
We needed to be able to keep it

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:33.000
and extend the rest of the
dinosaur in postproduction.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.615
And so it was really
important that it moved

00:15:34.615 --> 00:15:36.750
in the same way as our digital creature.

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:40.110
So this is the range of motion
in the physical animatronic

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:43.290
sufficient to allow the dinosaur
to do what it needs to do.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:45.960
We could actually pass that
information back to John Nolan,

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:47.790
who was the creature effects supervisor.

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:49.660
So when John was making the animatronics,

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:51.543
they would be positioned in such a way

00:15:51.543 --> 00:15:54.034
that matched ILM digital models.

00:15:54.034 --> 00:15:55.710
So it's kind of digital creatures

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:58.080
driving practical animation.

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:00.210
Narrator: To reflect
paleontological research,

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:02.458
the digital team also developed tools

00:16:02.458 --> 00:16:04.530
to create feathered dinosaurs.

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:07.050
David: Feathers has been
a long-burning desire,

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.810
I think, for all of the "Jurassic" fans.

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:12.300
Feathers are really
complicated to simulate

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:16.170
and to describe all of that
very intricate geometry in 3D.

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:19.433
So we actually built a
brand-new feather system at ILM

00:16:19.433 --> 00:16:22.320
in Houdini that we've
dubbed internally Quill.

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.780
So we were able to build
this procedural system

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:28.466
which gave the artist the
control over the groom

00:16:28.466 --> 00:16:30.241
and the texturing of those feathers

00:16:30.241 --> 00:16:33.666
that allowed simulation with snow, wind,

00:16:33.666 --> 00:16:36.513
water, all in the same software package.

00:16:36.513 --> 00:16:38.520
Narrator: Even with new
feathered creatures,

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:41.280
the dinosaurs created
by Stan Winston and ILM

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:45.240
nearly three decades ago are
still seen in today's movies.

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:48.210
For "Jurassic World: Dominion,"
director Colin Trevorrow

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:50.760
wanted the VFX creatures to feel as close

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:53.220
as possible to its predecessors.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:54.733
So the digital team conducted

00:16:54.733 --> 00:16:57.031
what they called digital archeology.

00:16:57.031 --> 00:17:00.098
David: The T. rex is where this
digital archeology comes in.

00:17:00.098 --> 00:17:03.647
We actually went back into
the ILM digital archives

00:17:03.647 --> 00:17:05.280
from the original "Jurassic Park,"

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:09.090
and we restored the original
3D Softimage models.

00:17:09.090 --> 00:17:10.828
So we were able to make sure that the

00:17:10.828 --> 00:17:12.444
model we're using from
"Jurassic World: Dominion"

00:17:12.444 --> 00:17:14.094
matched as closely as possible.

00:17:14.094 --> 00:17:15.870
And at the same time,
we actually went back

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:19.017
to Stan Winston's original
physical animatronic.

00:17:19.017 --> 00:17:20.942
And we have loads of really amazing

00:17:20.942 --> 00:17:22.410
reference photography of it,

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:25.410
and we completely retextured
the T. rex to match it.

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:28.230
Narrator: CG has come
a long way since then.

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:31.170
ILM only had 60 shots in "Jurassic."

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:33.540
Now we'll do several
thousand shots in a movie.

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:36.540
We are aware that these
walked the face of the Earth.

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:39.030
And for that, they have a
very, very special place.

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:40.890
And it also makes it
that much more exciting,

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:42.480
because you're kind of, you know,

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:45.797
"Wow, I'm bringing something
to life, a real thing to life."

